+1. Store encrypted ratchet sessions in Supabase (in device_keys or a new table)
+2. When a user logs in on a new device, fetch and restore their ratchet sessions
+3. Keep the existing encryption strength (AES-256-GCM)
+
+First, show me your analysis and proposed solution. Do NOT modify any files yet. Just explain your plan.

# 2026-08-09 18:51:22.976373
+IMPLEMENT THE E2E MULTI-DEVICE SESSION FIX ACCORDING TO THIS DESIGN.
+
+IMPORTANT:
+Work conservatively. Do not assume architecture that does not exist.
+Do not delete unrelated code.
+Do not modify files during Phase A.
+Do not create database migrations during Phase A.
+Do not implement the cloud-sync architecture until the current architecture has been fully analyzed and the design has been reported.
+
+PHASE A — FULL ANALYSIS (NO FILE MODIFICATIONS)
+
+Analyze the relevant E2E architecture in detail:
+
+1. artifacts/sovereign/src/utils/signalProtocol.ts
+   - Locate ratchetDecrypt.
+   - Confirm whether the message-key derivation incorrectly uses "send" instead of "recv".
+   - Explain how RatchetState is structured.
+   - Explain how sessions are created, saved, loaded, encrypted, decrypted, and advanced.
+   - Identify all callers of the relevant functions.
+
+2. artifacts/sovereign/src/utils/e2eManager.ts
+   - Explain how device identity keys are generated.
+   - Explain where private/public device keys are stored.
+   - Explain how device keys are registered in Supabase.
+   - Explain how deviceSecret is generated, derived, stored, and recovered.
+   - Identify whether any existing recovery/backup mechanism already exists.
+   - Explain how sessions are initialized and restored.
+
+3. artifacts/sovereign/src/integrations/supabase/types.ts
+   - Inspect the current database types.
+   - List the complete structure of device_keys.
+   - Search for any existing table suitable for encrypted session state.
+   - Do NOT create a new table yet.
+
+4. Search the entire repository for:
+   - RatchetState
+   - ratchetEncrypt
+   - ratchetDecrypt
+   - saveSession
+   - loadSession
+   - deviceSecret
+   - device_keys
+   - session
+   - E2E initialization
+   - encryption/decryption helpers
+   - AES-GCM utilities
+   - recovery/backup mechanisms
+
+5. Identify every relevant file and explain its role.
+
+6. Determine whether the current implementation already has:
+   - local session persistence
+   - cloud persistence
+   - multi-device support
+   - recovery-key support
+   - session versioning
+   - concurrency protection
+   - deferred synchronization
+
+PHASE A OUTPUT — DO NOT EDIT ANY FILES
+
+Provide a complete technical report containing:
+
+A. Current E2E architecture
+B. Current device architecture
+C. Current session architecture
+D. Current key-management architecture
+E. Current database architecture
+F. Confirmed bugs
+G. Missing functionality
+H. Security risks
+I. Performance risks
+J. Concurrency risks
+K. Files that must change
+L. Files that must NOT change
+M. Proposed database schema
+N. Proposed recovery-key architecture
+O. Proposed synchronization flow
+P. Proposed conflict-resolution flow
+
+IMPORTANT:
+Do not proceed to implementation until this Phase A report is complete.
+
+PHASE B — FIX THE CONFIRMED DECRYPTION BUG
+
+Only after Phase A confirms the bug:
+
+1. In signalProtocol.ts, inside ratchetDecrypt, change the message-key derivation information parameter from "send" to "recv" if and only if the analysis confirms this is the actual bug.
+
+2. Run the relevant type checks/tests/build checks.
+
+3. Do NOT commit until the fix has been verified.
+
+4. After verification, create a small isolated commit containing ONLY this bug fix.
+
+PHASE C — DESIGN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION
+
+Before creating any database table or modifying the synchronization architecture, present the final implementation design.
+
+The design MUST follow these principles:
+
+1. Local-first:
+   The local device remains the primary operational store.
+   Supabase is only an encrypted backup/synchronization layer.
+
+2. Recovery key:
+   The cloud backup must NOT depend exclusively on deviceSecret.
+   A new device must be able to obtain the required recovery material through a secure recovery mechanism.
+   Reuse an existing secure recovery mechanism if one already exists.
+   Do not invent a password/recovery architecture without first checking the existing implementation.
+
+3. Encryption:
+   Encrypt the session state using AES-256-GCM before uploading it.
+   The server must never receive plaintext Ratchet keys or plaintext RatchetState.
+
+4. Performance:
+   Do NOT synchronize after every message.
+   Use deferred synchronization with a debounce window of approximately 10–15 seconds.
+   Also attempt synchronization on visibilitychange/pagehide where appropriate.
+   Local operation must never depend on successful cloud synchronization.
+
+5. Concurrency:
+   Every synchronized session state must have a version.
+   Cloud updates must use optimistic concurrency / conditional updates.
+   A device must never blindly overwrite a newer cloud state.
+
+6. Conflict handling:
+   Do NOT merge two conflicting Ratchet states.
+   If a conflict occurs, detect it explicitly and re-evaluate/re-establish the session according to the existing E2E/session architecture.
+
+7. Device identity:
+   Do NOT copy private device identity keys from one device to another.
+   Each physical device keeps its own device identity.
+
+8. Database security:
+   Protect the cloud session table using RLS.
+   Users must only access their own records.
+   Never expose service-role credentials to the client.
+
+PHASE D — IMPLEMENTATION
+
+Only after Phase C has been presented and approved:
+
+1. If no suitable existing table exists, create/propose a ratchet_sessions table containing the minimum required fields, such as:
+
+   user_id
+   device_id
+   remote_user_id
+   remote_device_id
+   session_id
+   encrypted_session_state
+   state_version
+   updated_at
+
+   Adapt the exact schema to the existing database architecture instead of blindly copying this schema.
+
+2. Implement secure encryption/decryption of the cloud backup.
+
+3. Implement recovery-key handling using the existing recovery architecture if available.
+
+4. Implement deferred synchronization.
+
+5. Implement optimistic concurrency.
+
+6. Implement safe restoration on a new device.
+
+7. Update database types and migrations as required.
+
+8. Keep the changes minimal and localized.
+
+9. Do not rewrite unrelated E2E code.
+
+PHASE E — VALIDATION
+
+After implementation, test and report:
+
+1. Single-device encryption/decryption.
+2. Two devices belonging to the same user.
+3. New-device restoration.
+4. Offline → online synchronization.
+5. Deferred synchronization behavior.
+6. Two devices updating simultaneously.
+7. Newer cloud version protection.
+8. Recovery-key restoration.
+9. Server receives only encrypted session state.
+10. RLS prevents cross-user access.
+11. Revoked/deleted devices cannot obtain new session data.
+12. Out-of-order messages.
+13. Session re-establishment after a conflict.
+
+For every test, report:
+- PASS
+- FAIL
+- NOT TESTED
+
+Do not claim a scenario works unless it was actually verified.
+
+FINAL RULE:
+
+Before changing architecture, understand the existing architecture.
+
+Before creating a database table, inspect the existing database.
+
+Before creating a recovery-key mechanism, inspect existing key/recovery mechanisms.
+
+Before committing, test the change.
+
+Before claiming success, verify the behavior.
+
+START WITH PHASE A ONLY.
+DO NOT MODIFY ANY FILES YET.

