# 2026-08-17 17:20:09.633963
+We are performing a READ-ONLY forensic audit of the Sovereign application.
+
+IMPORTANT:
+- Do NOT modify any files.
+- Do NOT create files.
+- Do NOT delete files.
+- Do NOT run migrations.
+- Do NOT change the database.
+- Do NOT make commits.
+- Do NOT claim something is fixed.
+- This is an investigation only.
+
+You are auditing the CURRENT repository as it actually exists, not reconstructing what you think the project should contain.
+
+Your task is to understand the implementation deeply and produce a factual technical audit.
+
+Sovereign product invariants:
+
+1. Sovereign is a structured commercial communication system for talent.
+2. Every talent has an immutable unique username.
+3. The username is also the foundation of the public talent URL.
+4. Work, Private, and Public are separate inboxes.
+5. Work is ONLY for structured commercial Deal Cards and their resulting negotiations.
+6. A normal free-form message must NEVER bypass the Deal Card requirement for Work.
+7. A Deal Card must exist before a Work negotiation/conversation can begin.
+8. A company/user must be authenticated before sending a Deal Card.
+9. A Deal Card is sent to a specific talent.
+10. A talent may delegate Work management to one agent in the MVP.
+11. Agent delegation uses a generated link + code.
+12. The invitation/code expires after 15 minutes.
+13. The agent must authenticate and then gains access only to the talent's Work inbox.
+14. The agent must NOT gain access to the talent's Private or Public inboxes.
+15. The talent can revoke the agent.
+16. When revoked, the agent must immediately lose access to that talent's Work inbox.
+17. The Work inbox can be Open or Closed.
+18. Closed Work means new Deal Cards cannot be submitted to that talent.
+19. Private and Public inboxes also have Open/Closed behavior.
+20. Private access is manually controlled by the talent.
+21. Rejecting a Deal Card does not create a negotiation conversation.
+22. Accepting a Deal Card creates the Work conversation.
+23. The original Deal Card remains pinned/visible at the top of the negotiation.
+24. The company sees the talent and offer status, not the agent identity.
+25. The agent negotiates on behalf of the talent.
+26. An agent can switch between the talents they manage.
+27. The company has an offer/status view for offers it has sent.
+28. Deep links based on /@username are strategically important.
+29. The MVP does NOT include payments yet.
+30. Do not reintroduce abandoned AI classification, Golden Hour, or other legacy features.
+
+AUDIT THE FOLLOWING AREAS:
+
+A. Repository architecture
+- Identify the frontend entry points.
+- Identify routing.
+- Identify authentication code.
+- Identify profile/identity code.
+- Identify inbox/message code.
+- Identify Deal Card code.
+- Identify agent delegation code.
+- Identify agent dashboard/switching code.
+- Identify conversation/thread code.
+- Identify company offer tracking code.
+- Identify deep-link/public-profile code.
+- Identify Supabase integration.
+- Identify Edge Functions.
+- Identify database migrations.
+- Identify RLS/security policies.
+- Identify notification code.
+- Identify legacy/unused code.
+
+B. Authentication and identity
+- Determine exactly how signup works.
+- Determine exactly how login works.
+- Determine where display name is stored.
+- Determine where username is generated.
+- Determine whether username generation actually runs during signup.
+- Determine whether username uniqueness is enforced by the database.
+- Determine whether username is mutable.
+- Determine how the current authenticated user is identified throughout the app.
+
+C. Roles and authorization
+Do NOT assume there is a role enum.
+Determine from the actual code/database:
+- how a normal user is represented,
+- how a talent is represented,
+- how a company/sender is represented,
+- how an agent is represented,
+- how an agent-talent relationship is represented,
+- what permissions are derived from relationships.
+
+D. Agent delegation
+Trace the complete flow:
+Talent → generate invitation → link/code → agent authentication → code verification → manager relationship → Work access.
+
+Determine:
+- where the invitation is generated,
+- whether the token is cryptographically random,
+- where expiry is enforced,
+- whether the code is single-use,
+- whether replay is possible,
+- how acceptance creates the relationship,
+- how revocation works,
+- whether revocation immediately removes access,
+- whether an agent can access Private/Public,
+- whether one talent can accidentally have multiple active agents.
+
+E. Inbox architecture
+Determine exactly how Work, Private, and Public are represented.
+Trace:
+- database category,
+- message creation,
+- read status,
+- open/closed state,
+- access control,
+- frontend filtering,
+- backend enforcement,
+- RLS enforcement.
+
+Specifically determine whether a malicious/authenticated client could bypass the UI and insert/read messages it should not be allowed to.
+
+F. Deal Card lifecycle
+Trace the complete lifecycle from creation to negotiation.
+
+Determine:
+- all Deal Card fields,
+- required fields,
+- database constraints,
+- sender/recipient relationship,
+- statuses,
+- viewed/read behavior,
+- accept behavior,
+- reject behavior,
+- conversation creation,
+- pinned Deal Card behavior,
+- whether a Deal Card can be edited after sending,
+- whether a rejected Deal Card can accidentally open a conversation,
+- whether a Work conversation can exist without a Deal Card.
+
+G. Deep links
+Determine:
+- actual React Router routes,
+- /@username behavior,
+- public profile behavior,
+- action=deal behavior,
+- authentication interaction,
+- current reason deep links fail,
+- whether /compose or /search can conflict with the username route.
+
+H. Company offer tracker
+Determine:
+- where sent offers are queried,
+- which statuses are displayed,
+- whether Viewed is derived from read state,
+- whether Accepted/Rejected updates correctly,
+- whether talent identity is displayed correctly,
+- whether the page is actually reachable from navigation.
+
+I. Database and RLS
+Inspect the actual migrations and policies.
+For every important table, identify:
+- purpose,
+- primary key,
+- important foreign keys,
+- important constraints,
+- indexes,
+- RLS enabled/disabled,
+- policies,
+- security-sensitive gaps.
+
+Pay special attention to:
+profiles
+messages
+deal_cards
+message_limits
+direct_access
+manager_invitations
+manager_links
+device_keys
+ratchet_sessions
+
+J. Edge Functions
+Inspect all relevant Edge Functions and report:
+- purpose,
+- inputs,
+- authentication checks,
+- authorization checks,
+- CORS behavior,
+- error handling,
+- whether the function appears deployable,
+- whether frontend code actually calls it.
+
+K. Encryption
+Do NOT modify encryption code.
+Only determine:
+- what is currently encrypted,
+- where keys are handled,
+- whether ratchet_sessions is actually used,
+- whether multi-device sync exists,
+- whether encryption is part of the active MVP path or legacy/incomplete infrastructure.
+
+L. Legacy
+Identify code related to:
+- AI classification,
+- Gemini,
+- Golden Hour,
+- PilotPanel,
+- ScoutPanel,
+- useAI,
+- mock/dev authentication,
+- mockup-sandbox,
+- .agents,
+- old message-limit logic,
+- other abandoned features.
+
+Do not delete anything. Just report it.
+
+OUTPUT FORMAT:
+
+1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
+2. ACTUAL ARCHITECTURE
+3. AUTHENTICATION
+4. IDENTITY / USERNAME
+5. TALENT / AGENT / COMPANY MODEL
+6. INBOX ARCHITECTURE
+7. DEAL CARD ARCHITECTURE
+8. ACCEPT → CONVERSATION FLOW
+9. AGENT DELEGATION
+10. DEEP LINKS
+11. COMPANY OFFER TRACKER
+12. DATABASE / SUPABASE
+13. RLS / SECURITY
+14. EDGE FUNCTIONS
+15. ENCRYPTION
+16. LEGACY CODE
+17. CURRENT BUGS
+18. TECHNICAL DEBT
+19. PRODUCT REQUIREMENT vs IMPLEMENTATION GAPS
+20. CRITICAL RISKS
+21. UNKNOWN ITEMS THAT REQUIRE VERIFICATION
+22. RECOMMENDED ENGINEERING ORDER
+
+For every important conclusion, provide the actual file path and relevant symbol/function/component name.
+
+Classify every finding as exactly one of:
+
+CONFIRMED — directly verified from the repository.
+INFERRED — strongly suggested but not directly verified.
+UNKNOWN — cannot be established from the repository.
+CONFLICTING — repository behavior conflicts with the Sovereign product specification.
+
+Do not make assumptions silently.
+
+Most importantly:
+DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING.

